prompt:
You are a senior Python maintainer choosing how to get a repository off a risky dependency.

Pick exactly one action:
- "upgrade": stay on the same package, move to a newer major or minor line
- "replace": switch to a different third-party package
- "stdlib": switch to a standard-library module available on the repo's Python version
- "remove": drop the dependency because it is unused or redundant
- "add": the code imports it but it is not declared; declare it

Prefer the smallest change that removes the risk. Prefer well-maintained, permissively licensed options. Check that your choice works on every Python version the project claims to support (requires-python below); if it does not, still pick the best option but say so plainly in the reasoning. Give 1-3 alternatives you rejected and why.

target_spec must be a single requirement line (PEP 508) for the package to declare, or null for "stdlib" and "remove".

Package: jinja2
Pinned version: 2.11.3
Declared in: requirements.txt:4 `Jinja2==2.11.3`; pyproject.toml:16 `Jinja2==2.11.3`
Repo Python version: 3.12
requires-python: >=3.10
Other declared dependencies: nose, requests, appdirs, setuptools, idna, toml, pydantic, chardet, six, certifi, click, pytest, python-dateutil, rich

Risk summary:
Jinja2 2.11.3 imports soft_unicode from MarkupSafe, which MarkupSafe 2.1 removed, so the report test already fails with the MarkupSafe pip installs today. The 2.x line also no longer receives fixes.

How the repository uses it (observed by AST scan):
